# A function run on another thread and waited for, through the language's own
# `spawn` and `t.wait()` (spec § 11), under names of this package's own and
# with a pool that bounds how many run at once.
#
# The language carries any value across, whole, as a copy, and a mailbox
# carries values between running threads (`mailbox()`, `m.send(v)`,
# `m.receive(within_ms:)`, `m.close()`); this face keeps the two shapes its
# callers were written for, a thread of one `i64` and a thread of one `str`,
# and `run_all`, which no single call of the language is. A program that
# needs any other value, or threads that talk while they run, writes the
# language's forms themselves.
#
# A thread started by `start` is a `Thread`, by `start_text` a `TextThread`,
# and each is given back by `wait` or `wait_text`, which answer what its body
# returned. A thread never waited for stops the program when `main` returns,
# saying how many and where it was started; one waited for twice, itself or a
# copy, stops it at the second wait: both the language's own refusals. The
# runtime runs at most `max_running()` threads at once, and a start past them
# fails `limit`, so a program that starts one per input value goes through
# `run_all`, which bounds them.
#
# It does not sleep, which is `time`'s, nor count processors, which is
# `os.processor_count`.
#
# Not written yet, each a `pending` block of its ledger: whether a thread has
# ended, asked without waiting for it, or a wait bounded in time.

use threads/hero_os
use threads/internal/pool
use threads/internal/samples

## Codes

# The code of a thread the system, or the runtime's bound of threads running
# at once, refused to start: `start`, `start_sized`, `start_text`, `run_all`
# and `run_all_text` fail with it.
constant LIMIT: str
    pool.LIMIT

## Threads of a number

# A thread `start` or `start_sized` started, which the program owes `wait`.
# Only those two make one; every copy names the same thread, and the first
# `wait` spends them all.
record Thread
    # The language's own thread, answering one number.
    running: (thread i64)

# Starts `body` on `arg` on a thread of its own and answers it, running.
#
# - `body`: the function the thread runs, called once with `arg`; what it
#   returns is what `wait` answers. A body that aborts stops the program.
# - `arg`: the one value the thread is given, any `i64`.
#
# Fails `limit` where the system, or the runtime's bound of threads running at
# once, refuses one more. See also `wait`, `run_all`.
function start(body: (function(i64) -> i64), arg: i64) -> Thread?
    return start_sized(body: body, arg: arg, stack: 0)

# Starts `body` on `arg` on a thread with a stack of at least `stack` bytes,
# and answers it, running.
#
# - `body`: the function the thread runs, called once with `arg`; what it
#   returns is what `wait` answers.
# - `arg`: the one value the thread is given, any `i64`.
# - `stack`: the least size of the thread's stack, in bytes, for a body that
#   goes deep; 0 takes the runtime's own, which is at least the main
#   thread's.
#
# Fails `limit` where the system, or the runtime's bound of threads running at
# once, refuses one more. Aborts where `stack` is below zero. See also
# `start`.
function start_sized(body: (function(i64) -> i64), arg: i64, stack: i64) -> Thread?
    match spawn_sized(body, arg, bytes: stack)
        .ok running => return ok(Thread(running: running))
        .err e      => return fail(code: LIMIT, msg: e.msg)

# Waits for `thread` to end and answers what its body returned.
#
# - `thread`: the caller's thread, which this spends: neither it nor a copy
#   of it may be waited for again.
#
# Aborts where `thread`, or a copy of it, was waited for already. It blocks
# until the body returns, with no limit of time. See also `start`.
function wait(@thread: Thread) -> i64
    return thread.running.wait()

## Threads of a text

# A thread `start_text` started, which the program owes `wait_text`. Only
# `start_text` makes one; every copy names the same thread.
record TextThread
    # The language's own thread, answering one text.
    running: (thread str)

# Starts `body` on a copy of `arg` on a thread of its own and answers it,
# running.
#
# - `body`: the function the thread runs, called once with its own copy of
#   `arg`; a copy of what it returns is what `wait_text` answers.
# - `arg`: the one text the thread is given, copied, of any length; the empty
#   text crosses too.
#
# Fails `limit` where the system, or the runtime's bound of threads running at
# once, refuses one more. See also `wait_text`, `run_all_text`.
function start_text(body: (function(str) -> str), arg: str) -> TextThread?
    match spawn(body, arg)
        .ok running => return ok(TextThread(running: running))
        .err e      => return fail(code: LIMIT, msg: e.msg)

# Waits for `thread` to end and answers what its body returned.
#
# - `thread`: the caller's thread, which this spends: it may not be waited
#   for again.
#
# Aborts where `thread`, or a copy of it, was waited for already. It blocks
# until the body returns, with no limit of time. See also `start_text`.
function wait_text(@thread: TextThread) -> str
    return thread.running.wait()

## Many values

# `body` of each of `values`, each run on a thread, the answers in the order
# of `values`.
#
# - `body`: the function each thread runs, called once for each value.
# - `values`: the values, one thread each; an empty list starts none and
#   answers an empty list.
# - `workers`: at most how many threads run at once, from one; past
#   `max_running()` it is taken as that. `os.processor_count()` is the usual
#   choice.
#
# Every thread it starts is waited for before it answers, so nothing is owed
# after it. Fails `limit` where the system, or the runtime's bound, refuses a
# thread, once those it started have ended. Aborts where `workers` is below
# one. See also `start`, `run_all_text`.
function run_all(body: (function(i64) -> i64), values: [i64], workers: i64) -> [i64]?
    return pool.run_all(body: body, values: values, workers: workers)

# `body` of a copy of each of `values`, each run on a thread, the answers in
# the order of `values`.
#
# - `body`: the function each thread runs, called once for each value.
# - `values`: the texts, one thread each, copied; an empty list starts none.
# - `workers`: at most how many threads run at once, from one; past
#   `max_running()` it is taken as that.
#
# Every thread it starts is waited for before it answers. Fails `limit`
# where the system, or the runtime's bound, refuses a thread, once those it
# started have ended. Aborts where `workers` is below one. See also
# `start_text`, `run_all`.
function run_all_text(body: (function(str) -> str), values: [str], workers: i64) -> [str]?
    return pool.run_all_text(body: body, values: values, workers: workers)

# How many threads the runtime runs at once, the program's started and not
# yet waited for among them: past it a start fails `limit`.
#
# The same number on every platform. See also `run_all`.
function max_running() -> i64
    return hero_os.limit()

## Tests

test "start and wait: several at once, each answer to its own thread"
    first: Thread @= start(body: samples.square, arg: 3).must()
    second: Thread @= start(body: samples.sum_below, arg: 100000).must()
    third: Thread @= start(body: samples.same, arg: -1).must()
    assert wait(@third) == -1
    assert wait(@first) == 9
    assert wait(@second) == 4999950000

test "start: the largest and smallest i64 cross whole"
    smallest: i64 = -9223372036854775807 - 1
    largest: i64 = 9223372036854775807
    low: Thread @= start(body: samples.same, arg: smallest).must()
    high: Thread @= start(body: samples.same, arg: largest).must()
    assert wait(@low) == smallest
    assert wait(@high) == largest

test "start_sized: a stack the runtime's own, and one of eight megabytes"
    plain: Thread @= start_sized(body: samples.square, arg: 5, stack: 0).must()
    deep: Thread @= start_sized(body: samples.square, arg: 6, stack: 8388608).must()
    assert wait(@plain) == 25
    assert wait(@deep) == 36

test "start_text and wait_text: the empty text, text of more than one byte, several at once"
    empty: TextThread @= start_text(body: samples.shout, arg: "").must()
    wide: TextThread @= start_text(body: samples.same_text, arg: "caffè über 日本").must()
    assert wait_text(@wide) == "caffè über 日本"
    assert wait_text(@empty) == "!"

test "max_running: the runtime's ceiling, the same as its own door"
    assert max_running() == hero_os.limit()
    assert max_running() >= 1

## Examples

# A square computed on another thread; `samples.square` is
# `function square(n: i64) -> i64`, `n * n`.
test "start: a number squared on a thread of its own"
    t: Thread @= start(body: samples.square, arg: 12).must()
    assert wait(@t) == 144

# A thread started with room for a deep body; `samples.square` is
# `function square(n: i64) -> i64`, `n * n`.
test "start_sized: a thread with a stack of sixteen megabytes"
    t: Thread @= start_sized(body: samples.square, arg: 4, stack: 16777216).must()
    assert wait(@t) == 16

# The answer of a thread, waited for; `samples.sum_below` is
# `function sum_below(n: i64) -> i64`, the sum of 0 to `n` less one.
test "wait: the sum a thread computed"
    t: Thread @= start(body: samples.sum_below, arg: 5).must()
    assert wait(@t) == 10

# A text handled on another thread; `samples.shout` is
# `function shout(s: str) -> str`, `s + "!"`.
test "start_text: a word made a shout on a thread of its own"
    t: TextThread @= start_text(body: samples.shout, arg: "hello").must()
    assert wait_text(@t) == "hello!"

# The text a thread gave back; `samples.same_text` is
# `function same_text(s: str) -> str`, answering `s` as it came.
test "wait_text: a copy of what the thread returned"
    t: TextThread @= start_text(body: samples.same_text, arg: "è").must()
    assert wait_text(@t) == "è"

# Numbers squared four at a time, the answers in order; `samples.square` is
# `function square(n: i64) -> i64`, `n * n`.
test "run_all: squares of a list, four threads at a time"
    assert run_all(body: samples.square, values: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], workers: 4).must() == [1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36]

# Words handled two at a time, in order; `samples.shout` is
# `function shout(s: str) -> str`, `s + "!"`.
test "run_all_text: three words, two threads at a time"
    assert run_all_text(body: samples.shout, values: ["a", "b", "c"], workers: 2).must() == ["a!", "b!", "c!"]

# How many threads may run at once.
test "max_running: the runtime's ceiling"
    assert max_running() > 0
